        this._expanded = !this._expanded;

        const header = this.shadowRoot.querySelector(".header");
        this.shadowRoot.querySelector(".indicator").textContent =
          this._expanded ? "−" : "+";
        header.setAttribute("aria-expanded", String(this._expanded));
        this.shadowRoot.querySelector("#details").hidden = !this._expanded;

        const more = this.shadowRoot.querySelector(".more");
        more.hidden = this._expanded;
      };
    }

    connectedCallback() {
      this.shadowRoot.querySelector(".header")
        .addEventListener("click", this._toggle);
      this.shadowRoot.querySelector(".more")
        .addEventListener("click", this._toggle);
    }

    disconnectedCallback() {
      this.shadowRoot.querySelector(".header")
        .removeEventListener("click", this._toggle);
      this.shadowRoot.querySelector(".more")
        .removeEventListener("click", this._toggle);
    }
  }

  customElements.define("x-details", XDetails);
</script>
```

### Evaluation

Assistant B is better. Its implementation uses Shadow DOM and the requested slots, keeps the intro visible while collapsed, hides the details content until expanded, and provides the requested bordered, larger header and “expand more” button.

B does have a toggle bug: the indicator has its own click listener, and its click bubbles to the header’s listener. Clicking the indicator therefore toggles twice, leaving the component in its original state. The header is also a non-semantic `div`, so it lacks the built-in keyboard behavior and accessibility state of a button.

Assistant A has more serious problems that prevent it from working as described. The intro and expand button are inside a body whose collapsed styles hide it, while the details content is outside that body and remains visible. Its `transitionend` handler removes the expanded class, undoing the open state. Also, the nested update functions use `this.shadowRoot` without a bound `this`, causing an error when toggling. Finally, the icon code draws neither the requested plus nor minus.

Both answers would benefit from accessible disclosure state and keyboard-operable controls. B is closer to the requested behavior, despite its indicator double-toggle bug.

My final verdict: [[B>>A]]
